    subgroups the statement is just the contents of
    Problem~@pr:intersection-of-two-lie-subgroups. In the general case the
    subalgebra $inter.big frak(h)_nu$ coincides with the intersection of a
    finite number of subalgebras, say $frak(h)_(nu_1), ..., frak(h)_(nu_k)$, and
    by the above it is the tangent algebra of the Lie subgroup
    $hat(H) = H_(nu_1) inter dots.c inter H_(nu_k)$. For any $nu$ the Lie
    subgroup $hat(H) inter H_nu$ has the same tangent algebra as $hat(H)$. Hence
    $hat(H) inter H_nu$ is contained in $hat(H)$ and contains $hat(H)^0$ and
    therefore so is and does $H$. Thus $H$ is a Lie subgroup and its tangent
    algebra coincides with $inter.big frak(h)_nu$.]

  #hint[@pr:malcev-closure-of-ideal][Clearly, if $a$ is an automorphism of the
    Lie group $G$, then $(d a(frak(h)))^M = d a(frak(h)^M)$ for any Lie
    subalgebra $frak(h) subset frak(g)$. In particular,
    $((Ad g) frak(h))^M = (Ad g) frak(h)^M$. Further, use the fact that
    $frak(h)$ is an ideal if and only if $(Ad g) frak(h) = frak(h)$ for any
    $g in G^0$.]

  #hint[@pr:solvable-algebra-semidirect-sum][For an ideal take any subspace of
    codimension 1 containing the derived algebra and for a subalgebra take any
    complementary subspace.]

  #hint[@pr:weight-subspaces-independent][The proof is similar to that of the
    theorem on linear independence of eigensubspaces of a linear operator.]

  #hint[@pr:lie-theorem-triangular-basis][Take a one-dimensional invariant
    subspace $U subset V$ which exists due to the theorem and consider the
    quotient representation of $G$ in $V\/U$. Apply the theorem again to this
    representation, etc.]

  #hint[@pr:semisimple-no-commutative-ideals][Consider the last nonzero iterated
    derived algebra of $rad frak(g)$.]

  #hint[@pr:real-subspace-iff-conjugation-stable][If $overline(W) = W$ then with
    any $z = x + i y$ $(x, y in V)$ the subspace $W$ contains both
    $x = 1/2 (z + overline(z))$ and $y = 1/(2 i) (z - overline(z))$, which means
    that $W$ is the complexification of $U = W inter V$.]

  #hint[@pr:radical-complexification][Notice that $overline(rad frak(g)(CC))$ is
    a solvable ideal of $frak(g)(CC)$ hence is contained in $rad frak(g)(CC)$.]
